WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.636 --> 00:09.586 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference March 22,

00:09.586 --> 00:11.556 align:middle
2023 CHAIR POWELL.

00:11.726 --> 00:12.126 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:12.356 --> 00:13.746 align:middle
Before discussing
today's meeting,

00:13.746 --> 00:15.776 align:middle
let me briefly address
recent developments

00:15.776 --> 00:16.616 align:middle
in the banking sector.

00:17.916 --> 00:20.946 align:middle
In the past two weeks, serious
difficulties at a small number

00:20.946 --> 00:22.036 align:middle
of banks have emerged.

00:22.796 --> 00:25.386 align:middle
History has shown that
isolated banking problems,

00:25.386 --> 00:28.416 align:middle
if left unaddressed,
can undermine confidence

00:28.416 --> 00:30.146 align:middle
in healthy banks and
threaten the ability

00:30.146 --> 00:33.226 align:middle
of the banking system as a
whole to play its vital role

00:33.226 --> 00:35.286 align:middle
in supporting the
savings and credit needs

00:35.286 --> 00:36.586 align:middle
of households and businesses.

00:37.906 --> 00:39.716 align:middle
That is why, in response
to these events,

00:39.856 --> 00:42.136 align:middle
the Federal Reserve, working
with the Treasury Department

00:42.136 --> 00:44.896 align:middle
and the FDIC, took
decisive actions

00:44.896 --> 00:48.326 align:middle
to protect the U.S. economy and
to strengthen public confidence

00:48.326 --> 00:49.316 align:middle
in our banking system.

00:50.876 --> 00:53.706 align:middle
These actions demonstrate
that all depositors' savings

00:53.706 --> 00:55.366 align:middle
and the banking system are safe.

00:56.366 --> 00:57.496 align:middle
With the support
of the Treasury,

00:57.956 --> 01:00.326 align:middle
the Federal Reserve Board
created the Bank Term Funding

01:00.326 --> 01:03.436 align:middle
Program to ensure that
banks that hold safe

01:03.436 --> 01:06.686 align:middle
and liquid assets can, if
needed, borrow reserves

01:06.766 --> 01:08.466 align:middle
against those assets at par.

01:09.866 --> 01:12.876 align:middle
This program, along with our
long-standing discount window,

01:13.606 --> 01:15.966 align:middle
is effectively meeting
the unusual funding needs

01:15.966 --> 01:19.666 align:middle
that some banks have faced and
makes clear that ample liquidity

01:19.666 --> 01:20.976 align:middle
in the system is available.

01:22.096 --> 01:24.146 align:middle
Our banking system is
sound and resilient,

01:24.356 --> 01:25.976 align:middle
with strong capital
and liquidity.

01:27.286 --> 01:29.536 align:middle
We will continue to
closely monitor conditions

01:29.536 --> 01:32.366 align:middle
in the banking system and are
prepared to use all of our tools

01:32.746 --> 01:34.726 align:middle
as needed to keep
it safe and sound.

01:36.146 --> 01:38.256 align:middle
In addition, we are committed
to learning the lessons

01:38.256 --> 01:42.046 align:middle
from this episode and to work
to prevent episodes-events

01:42.046 --> 01:44.296 align:middle
like this from happening again.

01:46.056 --> 01:48.966 align:middle
Turning to the broader
economy and monetary policy:

01:49.596 --> 01:50.946 align:middle
Inflation remains too high,

01:50.946 --> 01:53.206 align:middle
and the labor market
continues to be very tight.

01:54.256 --> 01:56.176 align:middle
My colleagues and I
understand the hardship

01:56.226 --> 01:59.746 align:middle
that high inflation is causing,
and we remain strongly committed

01:59.746 --> 02:02.426 align:middle
to bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

02:03.616 --> 02:05.296 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

02:05.296 --> 02:06.086 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve.

02:06.786 --> 02:07.866 align:middle
Without price stability,

02:07.936 --> 02:09.746 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

02:10.336 --> 02:13.746 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

02:13.746 --> 02:15.676 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

02:15.676 --> 02:19.436 align:middle
of long-of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

02:19.976 --> 02:23.926 align:middle
The U.S. economy slowed
significantly last year,

02:24.076 --> 02:28.526 align:middle
with real GDP rising at a
below-trend pace of 0.9 percent.

02:29.706 --> 02:32.096 align:middle
Consumer spending appears to
have picked up this quarter,

02:32.366 --> 02:34.716 align:middle
although some of that strength
may reflect the effects

02:34.716 --> 02:37.156 align:middle
of swings in the weather
across the turn of the year.

02:38.346 --> 02:41.666 align:middle
In contrast, activity in the
housing sector remains weak,

02:42.346 --> 02:44.516 align:middle
largely reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

02:45.706 --> 02:48.636 align:middle
Higher interest rates and
slower output growth also appear

02:48.636 --> 02:51.466 align:middle
to be weighing on
business fixed investment.

02:52.656 --> 02:56.006 align:middle
Committee participants
generally expect subdued growth

02:56.006 --> 02:56.646 align:middle
to continue.

02:57.616 --> 02:59.916 align:middle
As shown in our Summary
of Economic Projections,

03:00.766 --> 03:04.126 align:middle
the median projection for
real GDP growth stands

03:04.126 --> 03:08.396 align:middle
at just 0.4 percent this year
and 1.2 percent next year,

03:09.096 --> 03:10.566 align:middle
well below the median estimate

03:10.746 --> 03:12.606 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

03:13.086 --> 03:18.056 align:middle
And nearly all participants
see the risks to GDP growth

03:18.056 --> 03:19.386 align:middle
as weighted to the downside.

03:20.776 --> 03:23.506 align:middle
Yet the labor market
remains extremely tight.

03:24.566 --> 03:26.426 align:middle
Job gains have picked
up in recent months,

03:26.426 --> 03:28.326 align:middle
with employment rising
by an average

03:28.326 --> 03:31.946 align:middle
of 351,000 jobs per month
over the last three months.

03:33.116 --> 03:36.516 align:middle
The unemployment rate remained
low in February at 3.6 percent.

03:36.996 --> 03:39.946 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate has edged

03:39.946 --> 03:44.186 align:middle
up in recent months, and wage
growth has shown some signs

03:44.186 --> 03:44.586 align:middle
of easing.

03:45.746 --> 03:48.456 align:middle
However, with job
vacancies still very high,

03:49.136 --> 03:51.946 align:middle
labor demand substantially
exceeds the supply

03:51.946 --> 03:53.006 align:middle
of available workers.

03:53.496 --> 03:58.256 align:middle
FOMC participants expect
supply and demand conditions

03:58.256 --> 04:01.006 align:middle
in the labor market to come
into better balance over time,

04:01.406 --> 04:03.816 align:middle
easing upward pressures
on wages and prices.

04:04.756 --> 04:07.676 align:middle
The median unemployment rate
projection in the SEP rises

04:07.676 --> 04:11.946 align:middle
to 4.5 percent at the end
of this year and 4.6 percent

04:11.946 --> 04:12.936 align:middle
at the end of next year.

04:15.326 --> 04:18.866 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

04:19.756 --> 04:22.086 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in January,

04:22.816 --> 04:25.796 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 5.4 percent;

04:26.496 --> 04:28.936 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories.

04:30.436 --> 04:35.236 align:middle
Core PCE-excluding those, core
PCE prices rose 4.7 percent.

04:35.706 --> 04:40.886 align:middle
In February, the 12 month change
in the CPI came in at 6 percent,

04:41.146 --> 04:44.066 align:middle
and the change in the
core CPI was 5.5 percent.

04:45.786 --> 04:48.636 align:middle
Inflation has moderated somewhat
since the middle of last year,

04:48.826 --> 04:51.116 align:middle
but the strength of these
recent readings indicates

04:51.466 --> 04:53.926 align:middle
that inflation pressures
continue to run high.

04:54.546 --> 04:57.056 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

04:57.216 --> 05:01.186 align:middle
for total PCE inflation is
3.3 percent for this year,

05:01.616 --> 05:06.146 align:middle
2.5 percent next year,
and 2.1 percent in 2025.

05:07.346 --> 05:09.126 align:middle
The process of getting
inflation back

05:09.126 --> 05:13.076 align:middle
down to 2 percent has a long way
to go and is likely to be bumpy.

05:15.416 --> 05:16.776 align:middle
Despite elevated inflation,

05:16.976 --> 05:19.856 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations appear

05:19.856 --> 05:23.036 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

05:23.036 --> 05:25.526 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

05:26.016 --> 05:28.096 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

05:28.656 --> 05:31.856 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

05:31.856 --> 05:34.136 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:34.536 --> 05:36.586 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

05:37.896 --> 05:39.646 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

05:39.646 --> 05:42.416 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

05:42.756 --> 05:46.456 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

05:46.456 --> 05:49.506 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

05:49.666 --> 05:50.916 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

05:52.266 --> 05:55.206 align:middle
We are highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

05:55.206 --> 05:58.256 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

05:58.256 --> 06:00.596 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

06:01.126 --> 06:03.246 align:middle
At today's meeting,

06:03.246 --> 06:05.776 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

06:05.826 --> 06:08.546 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point,
bringing the target range

06:08.546 --> 06:10.346 align:middle
to 4  to 5 percent.

06:11.486 --> 06:12.866 align:middle
And we are continuing
the process

06:12.866 --> 06:15.896 align:middle
of significantly reducing
our securities holdings.

06:17.316 --> 06:19.126 align:middle
Since our previous FOMC meeting,

06:19.126 --> 06:21.796 align:middle
economic indicators have
generally come in stronger

06:21.796 --> 06:24.466 align:middle
than expected, demonstrating
greater momentum

06:24.466 --> 06:26.606 align:middle
in economic activity
and inflation.

06:27.936 --> 06:30.186 align:middle
We believe, however, that
events in the banking system

06:30.186 --> 06:33.396 align:middle
over the past two weeks
are likely to result

06:33.596 --> 06:36.036 align:middle
in tighter credit conditions
for households and businesses,

06:36.606 --> 06:38.786 align:middle
which would in turn
affect economic outcomes.

06:39.716 --> 06:42.266 align:middle
It is too soon to determine
the extent of these effects

06:42.376 --> 06:44.816 align:middle
and therefore too soon to
tell how monetary policy

06:44.816 --> 06:45.516 align:middle
should respond.

06:46.596 --> 06:49.446 align:middle
As a result, we no longer
state that we anticipate

06:49.446 --> 06:51.786 align:middle
that ongoing rate
increases will be appropriate

06:51.786 --> 06:54.726 align:middle
to quell inflation;
instead, we now anticipate

06:54.726 --> 06:57.216 align:middle
that some additional policy
firming may be appropriate.

06:57.966 --> 07:00.446 align:middle
We will closely monitor
incoming data

07:00.446 --> 07:03.546 align:middle
and carefully assess the
actual and expected effects

07:03.546 --> 07:05.806 align:middle
of tighter credit conditions
on economic activity,

07:06.386 --> 07:07.896 align:middle
the labor market, and inflation,

07:09.176 --> 07:11.436 align:middle
and our policy decisions
will reflect that assessment.

07:13.296 --> 07:15.706 align:middle
In our SEP, each FOMC
participant wrote

07:15.706 --> 07:19.076 align:middle
down an appropriate path for the
federal funds rate based on what

07:19.076 --> 07:20.226 align:middle
that participant judges

07:20.226 --> 07:22.566 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario going forward.

07:24.276 --> 07:26.686 align:middle
If the economy evolves
as projected,

07:26.946 --> 07:29.986 align:middle
the median participant projects
that the appropriate level

07:29.986 --> 07:32.946 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 5.1 percent at the end

07:32.946 --> 07:36.426 align:middle
of this year, 4.3 percent
at the end of 2024,

07:37.186 --> 07:39.956 align:middle
and 3.1 percent at
the end of 2025.

07:40.566 --> 07:43.266 align:middle
These are little changed from
our December projections,

07:43.636 --> 07:45.276 align:middle
reflecting offsetting factors.

07:46.726 --> 07:49.126 align:middle
These projections are not a
Committee decision or plan;

07:49.186 --> 07:51.586 align:middle
if the economy does not
evolve as projected,

07:51.856 --> 07:54.226 align:middle
the path for policy will
adjust as appropriate

07:54.786 --> 07:57.716 align:middle
to foster our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

07:58.796 --> 08:01.176 align:middle
We will continue to make our
meeting-decisions meeting

08:01.176 --> 08:03.856 align:middle
by meeting, based on the
totality of the vincoming data

08:03.856 --> 08:06.556 align:middle
and their implications
for the outlook

08:06.556 --> 08:08.346 align:middle
for economic activity
and inflation.

08:08.876 --> 08:12.146 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

08:12.146 --> 08:13.456 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

08:13.456 --> 08:16.166 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

08:16.166 --> 08:16.776 align:middle
well anchored.

08:17.826 --> 08:20.256 align:middle
Reducing inflation is
likely to require a period

08:20.256 --> 08:22.606 align:middle
of below-trend growth
and some softening

08:22.636 --> 08:23.826 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

08:24.866 --> 08:27.446 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

08:27.446 --> 08:29.356 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

08:29.356 --> 08:32.336 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

08:33.536 --> 08:36.786 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

08:36.786 --> 08:38.656 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

08:39.206 --> 08:41.626 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:42.436 --> 08:44.136 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:44.606 --> 08:46.056 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:46.236 --> 08:47.466 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:47.896 --> 08:48.386 align:middle
Thank you.

08:48.726 --> 08:50.296 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

08:50.706 --> 08:52.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:52.686 --> 08:53.736 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

08:55.066 --> 08:55.556 align:middle
Thank you.

08:55.826 --> 08:57.546 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

08:57.656 --> 09:00.796 align:middle
How confident is the Committee
that the recent stress

09:00.886 --> 09:03.416 align:middle
that we've seen, and you've
alluded to, is contained

09:03.416 --> 09:07.336 align:middle
at this point and that deposit
flight among midsize lenders

09:07.336 --> 09:09.166 align:middle
in particular has ceased?

09:09.506 --> 09:10.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:10.446 --> 09:12.416 align:middle
Thanks. So I, I guess
our view is

09:12.416 --> 09:14.076 align:middle
that the banking system is sound

09:14.076 --> 09:16.356 align:middle
and it's resilient-it's got
strong capital [and] liquidity.

09:17.226 --> 09:20.126 align:middle
We took powerful actions with
[the] Treasury and the FDIC,

09:20.126 --> 09:23.246 align:middle
which demonstrate that all
depositors' savings are safe

09:23.496 --> 09:24.906 align:middle
and that the banking
system is safe.

09:25.726 --> 09:28.116 align:middle
Deposit flows in the banking
system have stabilized

09:28.116 --> 09:28.926 align:middle
over the last week.

09:29.666 --> 09:31.376 align:middle
And the last thing I'll say is

09:31.376 --> 09:34.346 align:middle
that we've undertaken-we're
undertaking a thorough internal

09:34.346 --> 09:35.326 align:middle
review that will identify

09:35.326 --> 09:37.306 align:middle
where we can strengthen
supervision and regulation.

09:38.036 --> 09:38.346 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

09:38.346 --> 09:40.476 align:middle
Okay, just a quick follow-up:
I mean, given all the stress

09:40.606 --> 09:42.326 align:middle
and the uncertainty
that you've also alluded

09:42.326 --> 09:43.116 align:middle
to in the statement,

09:43.396 --> 09:46.536 align:middle
how seriously was a-was a pause
considered for this meeting?

09:46.986 --> 09:47.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:47.256 --> 09:50.886 align:middle
So we considered-we did consider
that in the days running

09:50.886 --> 09:53.666 align:middle
up to the meeting, and you
see the decision that we made,

09:53.666 --> 09:56.936 align:middle
which I'll say a
couple things about.

09:57.376 --> 09:59.836 align:middle
First, it was supported by
a very strong consensus,

09:59.836 --> 10:01.226 align:middle
and I'll be happy
to explain why.

10:01.826 --> 10:05.066 align:middle
And, really, it is that the
intermeeting data on inflation

10:05.126 --> 10:07.666 align:middle
and the labor market came in
stronger than expected and,

10:07.666 --> 10:10.706 align:middle
really, before the recent
events, we were clearly on track

10:10.706 --> 10:12.376 align:middle
to continue with
ongoing rate hikes.

10:12.756 --> 10:15.446 align:middle
In fact, as of a couple of weeks
ago, it looked like we'd need

10:15.446 --> 10:17.866 align:middle
to raise rates over the
course of the year more

10:17.866 --> 10:20.616 align:middle
than we had expected
at the time of the SEP

10:20.616 --> 10:22.436 align:middle
in December-at the time
of the December meeting.

10:23.556 --> 10:25.426 align:middle
We are committed to
restoring price stability,

10:25.426 --> 10:28.636 align:middle
and all of the evidence says
that the public has confidence

10:28.636 --> 10:30.726 align:middle
that we will do so-that
we'll bring inflation

10:30.726 --> 10:32.636 align:middle
down to 2 percent over time.

10:33.176 --> 10:35.626 align:middle
It is important that we
sustain that confidence

10:36.076 --> 10:38.136 align:middle
with our actions as
well as our words.

10:39.586 --> 10:42.166 align:middle
So we also assess, as I
mentioned, that the events

10:42.166 --> 10:44.016 align:middle
of the last two weeks
are likely to result

10:44.016 --> 10:46.216 align:middle
in some tightening credit
conditions for households

10:46.216 --> 10:48.206 align:middle
and businesses and
thereby weigh on demand,

10:48.296 --> 10:50.676 align:middle
on the labor market,
and on inflation.

10:51.216 --> 10:54.976 align:middle
Such a tightening in
financial conditions would work

10:54.976 --> 10:56.566 align:middle
in the same direction
as rate tightening.

10:56.566 --> 10:58.436 align:middle
In principle, as a matter
of fact, you can think of it

10:58.436 --> 11:01.596 align:middle
as being the equivalent of
a rate hike or perhaps more

11:01.596 --> 11:04.016 align:middle
than that; of course,
it's not possible to make

11:04.016 --> 11:06.936 align:middle
that assessment today with
any precision whatsoever.

11:07.556 --> 11:09.016 align:middle
So our decision was
to move ahead

11:09.016 --> 11:12.866 align:middle
with the 25 basis point hike
and to change our guidance,

11:12.866 --> 11:15.846 align:middle
as I mentioned, from
ongoing hikes to some,

11:16.566 --> 11:19.736 align:middle
some additional hikes
maybe-some policy firming may

11:19.736 --> 11:20.356 align:middle
be appropriate.

11:21.286 --> 11:24.376 align:middle
So going forward,
as I mentioned,

11:24.376 --> 11:27.166 align:middle
in assessing the need for, for
further hikes, we'll be focused

11:27.166 --> 11:30.836 align:middle
as always on the incoming data
and the evolving outlook and,

11:30.836 --> 11:33.006 align:middle
in particular, on our
assessment of the actual

11:33.006 --> 11:34.536 align:middle
and expected effects
of credit tightening.

11:36.446 --> 11:37.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:38.896 --> 11:40.446 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

11:40.836 --> 11:44.186 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, can you
explain the difference

11:44.186 --> 11:47.606 align:middle
between ongoing rate
increases and firming?

11:48.816 --> 11:51.276 align:middle
Does firming imply a
rate increase per se,

11:51.276 --> 11:53.926 align:middle
or could policy firm
without you increasing rates?

11:54.426 --> 11:55.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:56.006 --> 11:58.606 align:middle
No, I think it's, it's meant
to refer to our policy rate.

11:58.606 --> 12:01.316 align:middle
Really, I would focus on, on
the words "may" and "some,"

12:02.166 --> 12:03.746 align:middle
as opposed to "ongoing."

12:04.666 --> 12:07.326 align:middle
Ongoing. So we, we clearly
were-what we were doing there

12:07.326 --> 12:10.256 align:middle
was taking onboard the-trying
to reflect the uncertainty

12:10.716 --> 12:12.176 align:middle
about what will happen.

12:12.176 --> 12:14.596 align:middle
I mean, it, it's possible
that this will turn

12:14.596 --> 12:16.886 align:middle
out to have very modest
effects-that these events will

12:16.966 --> 12:19.286 align:middle
turn out to be very, very
modest effects on the economy,

12:19.616 --> 12:22.736 align:middle
in which case inflation will
continue to be strong-in

12:22.736 --> 12:26.726 align:middle
which case, you know, the path
will look-might look different.

12:26.896 --> 12:30.066 align:middle
It's also possible that this
potential tightening will

12:30.066 --> 12:33.916 align:middle
contribute significant
tightening in credit conditions

12:33.916 --> 12:37.326 align:middle
over time and, in
principle, if that-that means

12:37.326 --> 12:39.546 align:middle
that monetary policy may
have less work to do.

12:39.546 --> 12:40.656 align:middle
We simply don't know.

12:41.246 --> 12:42.646 align:middle
So. STEVE LIESMAN.

12:42.646 --> 12:44.836 align:middle
Do you have concerns that the
recent-that the hike you did

12:44.836 --> 12:47.076 align:middle
today could further exacerbate
the problem in the banks?

12:47.246 --> 12:47.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:48.676 --> 12:50.976 align:middle
No. I mean, with our
monetary policy, we're,

12:50.976 --> 12:53.806 align:middle
we're really focused on
macroeconomic outcomes.

12:53.806 --> 12:55.206 align:middle
In particular, we're focused on,

12:55.206 --> 12:59.016 align:middle
on this potential credit
tightening and what can

12:59.016 --> 13:01.686 align:middle
that produce in the way of
tighter credit conditions.

13:02.026 --> 13:04.546 align:middle
I think when we think about
the situation with the banks,

13:04.546 --> 13:08.026 align:middle
we're focused on our-on our
financial stability tools,

13:08.026 --> 13:12.466 align:middle
in particular our lending
facilities, the debt-sorry,

13:12.736 --> 13:15.016 align:middle
the discount window, and
also the new facility.

13:15.466 --> 13:16.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:17.466 --> 13:22.346 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:23.016 --> 13:24.306 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

13:24.706 --> 13:26.686 align:middle
Chair Powell, in your
testimony two weeks ago,

13:26.686 --> 13:28.616 align:middle
you had indicated you thought
the terminal rate would

13:28.616 --> 13:29.166 align:middle
be higher.

13:29.166 --> 13:31.656 align:middle
Obviously, that was before the
stress in the banking sector.

13:32.226 --> 13:33.816 align:middle
And I realize there's
a lot of uncertainty,

13:33.816 --> 13:35.586 align:middle
but can you-can you
explain at all

13:35.586 --> 13:39.326 align:middle
to what extent your forecasts
or those of your colleagues

13:39.326 --> 13:42.996 align:middle
or those of the Board staff
incorporated today a material

13:42.996 --> 13:44.436 align:middle
tightening of credit
availability

13:44.846 --> 13:46.766 align:middle
because of the stress
in the banking sector,

13:47.056 --> 13:52.306 align:middle
or are you waiting to see it in
the data before you incorporate

13:52.686 --> 13:56.196 align:middle
that potential tightening
into your forecasts?

13:56.676 --> 13:57.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:57.266 --> 13:59.756 align:middle
So, you know, we have just
come from an FOMC meeting and,

14:00.286 --> 14:02.616 align:middle
you know, the people who
write the minutes will be very

14:02.616 --> 14:04.506 align:middle
carefully counting, but
I'll tell you what I heard.

14:04.656 --> 14:07.646 align:middle
What I heard was significant
number of people saying

14:07.646 --> 14:10.906 align:middle
that they anticipated there
would be some, some tightening

14:10.906 --> 14:14.106 align:middle
of credit conditions and that
would really have the same

14:14.106 --> 14:16.006 align:middle
effects as, as our policies do,

14:16.476 --> 14:18.366 align:middle
and that therefore
they were including

14:18.366 --> 14:20.946 align:middle
that in their assessment and
that if that did turn out not

14:20.946 --> 14:23.356 align:middle
to be the case, in principle
you would need more rate hikes.

14:23.496 --> 14:25.856 align:middle
So some people did reflect

14:26.356 --> 14:30.896 align:middle
that in their FOMC-in
their SEP forecasts.

14:31.136 --> 14:32.776 align:middle
I think there may also
just have been-remember,

14:32.776 --> 14:34.036 align:middle
this is 12 days ago.

14:34.606 --> 14:37.686 align:middle
You know, we're trying to assess
something that just is so recent

14:38.176 --> 14:40.926 align:middle
and it's people-you know,
it's very difficult,

14:40.926 --> 14:42.046 align:middle
there's so much uncertainty.

14:42.046 --> 14:44.576 align:middle
So December was a good
place to start, and we wound

14:44.576 --> 14:47.626 align:middle
up with-we wound up with very
similar outcomes for December.

14:48.606 --> 14:51.396 align:middle
And, you know, in a
way, the early-the data

14:51.696 --> 14:53.506 align:middle
in the first part-the
first five weeks

14:53.506 --> 14:56.526 align:middle
of the intermeeting period
pointed to stronger inflation

14:56.526 --> 14:57.586 align:middle
and stronger labor markets.

14:57.586 --> 14:59.646 align:middle
So that pointed to higher rates.

14:59.706 --> 15:03.536 align:middle
And then this, this latter
part kind of-the possibility

15:04.156 --> 15:06.706 align:middle
of credit conditions
tightening really,

15:06.706 --> 15:07.926 align:middle
really offset that, effectively.

15:07.926 --> 15:08.616 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:08.616 --> 15:09.816 align:middle
To follow up: Have
you considered

15:09.816 --> 15:11.766 align:middle
at all whether your primary
tool, the funds rate,

15:12.206 --> 15:14.416 align:middle
is going to be enough
to sustain the kind

15:14.416 --> 15:15.886 align:middle
of tighter financial conditions

15:16.306 --> 15:17.656 align:middle
that you believe
will be necessary

15:17.656 --> 15:21.036 align:middle
without doing significant
damage to the banking sector?

15:21.036 --> 15:21.816 align:middle
Have you, for example,

15:21.816 --> 15:24.866 align:middle
considered changing reserve
requirements, selling assets

15:24.866 --> 15:26.376 align:middle
out of the System
Open Market Account,

15:26.786 --> 15:29.966 align:middle
as a way to better achieve
tighter financial conditions

15:30.306 --> 15:33.356 align:middle
that don't accelerate
deposit erosion,

15:33.356 --> 15:34.576 align:middle
for example, from banks?

15:34.956 --> 15:35.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:35.476 --> 15:38.526 align:middle
You know, we know that we have
other, other tools in effect,

15:38.526 --> 15:41.426 align:middle
but no, we think our monetary
policy tool works, and we think,

15:41.426 --> 15:47.996 align:middle
you know, many, many banks-our
rate hikes were well telegraphed

15:47.996 --> 15:50.856 align:middle
to the market, and many banks
have managed to handle them.

15:51.656 --> 15:52.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:53.136 --> 15:55.366 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

15:55.506 --> 15:57.816 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

15:57.816 --> 16:00.306 align:middle
I wanted to ask-you,
along with the FDIC

16:00.306 --> 16:02.296 align:middle
and the Treasury-the
Fed Board decided

16:02.296 --> 16:04.316 align:middle
to invoke the systemic
risk exception

16:04.666 --> 16:07.986 align:middle
to allow uninsured depositors to
be protected at these two banks.

16:07.986 --> 16:10.576 align:middle
I was just wondering if
you could speak to why

16:10.576 --> 16:11.506 align:middle
that decision was made.

16:11.506 --> 16:14.976 align:middle
Was it purely a confidence
issue, or was there a concern

16:15.306 --> 16:18.066 align:middle
that there would be some
sort of economic contagion

16:18.066 --> 16:20.236 align:middle
or financial contagion from
the failure of these banks?

16:20.236 --> 16:21.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:21.166 --> 16:24.026 align:middle
The issue was really not
about those specific banks,

16:24.056 --> 16:28.306 align:middle
but about the risk of a
contagion to, to other banks

16:28.306 --> 16:30.356 align:middle
and to the financial
markets more broadly.

16:30.856 --> 16:31.386 align:middle
That was the issue.

16:31.496 --> 16:32.096 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

16:32.096 --> 16:34.856 align:middle
Okay, and then can you also-just
to follow up, can you speak

16:34.856 --> 16:36.316 align:middle
to the role that
you will be playing

16:36.316 --> 16:37.936 align:middle
in the Fed's internal
investigation

16:38.136 --> 16:39.596 align:middle
on its supervision
and regulation?

16:39.836 --> 16:40.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:40.336 --> 16:43.166 align:middle
So Vice Chair Barr is, is of
course leading that review.

16:43.746 --> 16:46.986 align:middle
And he's responsible
for it in his capacity

16:46.986 --> 16:48.216 align:middle
as Vice Chair for Supervision.

16:49.296 --> 16:54.416 align:middle
We-I realized, you know, right
away that, that there was going

16:54.416 --> 16:55.476 align:middle
to be a need for review.

16:56.146 --> 16:57.886 align:middle
I mean, the question
we're all asking ourselves

16:57.886 --> 16:59.666 align:middle
over that first weekend
was, how did this happen?

17:00.336 --> 17:05.896 align:middle
And so what we did was, early
Monday morning, we sat down

17:05.896 --> 17:06.756 align:middle
and said, "Let's do this."

17:06.756 --> 17:09.776 align:middle
And he, he was obviously going
to lead it in his capacity.

17:09.776 --> 17:17.086 align:middle
So I don't-my role was to
announce it, and I get briefed

17:17.086 --> 17:20.806 align:middle
on it, but I'm not involved
in, in the work of it.

17:21.856 --> 17:22.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:23.106 --> 17:23.936 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

17:25.126 --> 17:26.086 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

17:26.086 --> 17:27.096 align:middle
Howard Schneider from Reuters.

17:27.466 --> 17:29.896 align:middle
So I want to go back to your
February press conference.

17:29.896 --> 17:31.856 align:middle
You mentioned the word
"disinflation," I believe,

17:31.856 --> 17:33.726 align:middle
9 or 10 times, a process

17:33.766 --> 17:37.956 align:middle
that you felt was-I forget the
word you used-but gratefully

17:37.956 --> 17:39.276 align:middle
under way, or something
like that.

17:39.676 --> 17:41.866 align:middle
Is disinflation still
occurring in the U.S. today?

17:42.196 --> 17:42.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:42.556 --> 17:44.576 align:middle
Yes. I mean, what
actually happened, Howard,

17:44.576 --> 17:45.996 align:middle
was I got the question 12 times,

17:45.996 --> 17:48.886 align:middle
so it's-maybe it's a
feature, not a bug.

17:48.946 --> 17:55.206 align:middle
But, so, but yeah, absolutely
the same-the story is intact,

17:55.206 --> 17:57.056 align:middle
so it's really three
parts, right?

17:57.166 --> 17:59.606 align:middle
Goods inflation has been
coming down now for six months;

18:00.086 --> 18:02.926 align:middle
it's proceeding more slowly
than we would have liked,

18:03.036 --> 18:04.406 align:middle
but it's certainly proceeding.

18:04.986 --> 18:08.326 align:middle
Housing services is, is really
a matter of time passing.

18:08.326 --> 18:10.756 align:middle
We continue to see the
new leases being signed

18:10.756 --> 18:12.616 align:middle
at much lower levels
of inflation.

18:12.616 --> 18:16.396 align:middle
So that's 44 percent of
the-of the core PCE index,

18:16.396 --> 18:18.046 align:middle
where you've got a
story that's ongoing.

18:18.466 --> 18:19.736 align:middle
Where we didn't have
in February,

18:19.736 --> 18:23.436 align:middle
and we still don't have
now, is a sign of progress

18:23.516 --> 18:26.126 align:middle
in the nonhousing
services sector.

18:26.646 --> 18:29.966 align:middle
And that is, you know, that's
just something that will have

18:29.966 --> 18:33.866 align:middle
to come through softening demand
and perhaps some softening

18:33.866 --> 18:34.866 align:middle
in labor market conditions.

18:34.866 --> 18:36.256 align:middle
We don't see that yet.

18:36.396 --> 18:38.746 align:middle
And that's, that's of course
56 percent of the index.

18:39.036 --> 18:40.856 align:middle
So the story is pretty
much the same.

18:41.046 --> 18:43.606 align:middle
I will say that the inflation
data that we got, to your point,

18:44.176 --> 18:45.766 align:middle
really pointed to
stronger inflation.

18:45.766 --> 18:46.306 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

18:46.476 --> 18:47.986 align:middle
If I could follow up on that,

18:47.986 --> 18:49.896 align:middle
I was curious why you
don't see more coming

18:49.936 --> 18:52.416 align:middle
from the credit crunch, because
it seems to me that's something

18:52.856 --> 18:56.716 align:middle
that you'd actually welcome
to a degree and expect.

18:56.716 --> 18:59.166 align:middle
And are you not seeing
more coming from that

18:59.166 --> 19:02.616 align:middle
because you don't know, or
because you just don't want

19:02.616 --> 19:03.996 align:middle
to have another round
of wishful thinking?

19:04.056 --> 19:04.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:04.286 --> 19:07.206 align:middle
So it's really just a question
of not knowing at this point.

19:07.206 --> 19:10.576 align:middle
There's a great deal of
literature on the connection

19:10.576 --> 19:13.706 align:middle
between tighter credit
conditions, economic activity,

19:13.706 --> 19:14.776 align:middle
hiring, and inflation.

19:15.306 --> 19:16.976 align:middle
Very large body of literature.

19:17.396 --> 19:20.246 align:middle
The question is, how significant
will this credit tightening be

19:20.726 --> 19:22.276 align:middle
and how sustainable it will be?

19:22.276 --> 19:23.256 align:middle
That's, that's the issue.

19:23.616 --> 19:25.356 align:middle
And we don't really
see it yet, so,

19:25.356 --> 19:26.986 align:middle
so people are making
estimates, you know,

19:26.986 --> 19:30.176 align:middle
people are publishing
estimates, but it's very kind

19:30.176 --> 19:33.226 align:middle
of rule-of-thumb guesswork
almost at this point.

19:33.496 --> 19:35.926 align:middle
But we think it's, it's
potentially quite real

19:36.476 --> 19:40.266 align:middle
and that argues for, you know,
being alert as we go forward.

19:40.266 --> 19:42.576 align:middle
As we think about
further rate hikes for us,

19:42.756 --> 19:44.816 align:middle
we'll be paying attention
to the actual

19:44.816 --> 19:47.546 align:middle
and expected effects from that.

19:48.536 --> 19:49.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:49.926 --> 19:52.396 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

19:52.566 --> 19:53.186 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

19:53.186 --> 19:54.356 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

19:54.356 --> 19:55.706 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

19:55.746 --> 19:58.616 align:middle
I wonder if you could
talk a little bit-I know

19:58.616 --> 20:00.946 align:middle
that you've got your internal
review coming, but I wonder

20:00.946 --> 20:03.716 align:middle
if you could talk a little bit
about what you think happened

20:03.716 --> 20:06.966 align:middle
with oversight at Silicon Valley
Bank and whether this suggests

20:07.176 --> 20:09.296 align:middle
that something about
regulation and supervision needs

20:09.296 --> 20:11.016 align:middle
to actually change
going forward.

20:11.286 --> 20:13.516 align:middle
And I wonder, you know, how
can the American people have

20:13.516 --> 20:16.086 align:middle
confidence that there aren't
other weaknesses out there

20:16.086 --> 20:17.066 align:middle
in the banking system,

20:17.066 --> 20:19.456 align:middle
given that this one got
missed, as you noted?

20:19.686 --> 20:20.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:20.246 --> 20:23.716 align:middle
So let me say what, what I think
happened, and then I'll come

20:23.716 --> 20:26.106 align:middle
to the questions
around supervision.

20:26.536 --> 20:31.466 align:middle
So, at a basic level, Silicon
Valley Bank management failed

20:31.556 --> 20:35.206 align:middle
badly-they grew the bank very
quickly, they exposed the bank

20:35.206 --> 20:37.936 align:middle
to significant liquidity
risk and interest rate risk,

20:38.426 --> 20:39.516 align:middle
didn't hedge that risk.

20:39.976 --> 20:45.656 align:middle
We now know that supervisors saw
these risks and, and intervened.

20:46.326 --> 20:50.026 align:middle
We know that the
public saw all this.

20:50.026 --> 20:53.546 align:middle
We know that SVB experienced
an unprecedentedly rapid

20:53.586 --> 20:54.696 align:middle
and massive bank run.

20:54.846 --> 20:56.816 align:middle
So this is a-this is
a very large group

20:56.816 --> 20:58.996 align:middle
of connected
depositors-concentrated group

20:58.996 --> 21:02.046 align:middle
of connected depositors
in a very, very fast run,

21:02.046 --> 21:05.106 align:middle
faster than historical
record would suggest.

21:05.566 --> 21:12.486 align:middle
So as for us-so for our
part, we're doing a review

21:12.536 --> 21:14.336 align:middle
of supervision and regulation.

21:15.216 --> 21:19.326 align:middle
My only interest is that we
identify what went wrong here.

21:19.706 --> 21:21.306 align:middle
How did this happen
is the question.

21:21.496 --> 21:22.236 align:middle
What went wrong?

21:22.946 --> 21:23.726 align:middle
Try to find that.

21:23.866 --> 21:24.666 align:middle
We will find that.

21:24.876 --> 21:27.086 align:middle
And then make an assessment
of what are the right policies

21:27.086 --> 21:28.686 align:middle
to put in place so that
it doesn't happen again,

21:28.686 --> 21:30.316 align:middle
and then implement
those policies.

21:31.376 --> 21:35.476 align:middle
It would be inappropriate for me
at this stage to offer my views

21:35.476 --> 21:36.696 align:middle
on what the answers might be.

21:36.886 --> 21:38.236 align:middle
You know, I simply
can't do that.

21:38.236 --> 21:39.636 align:middle
Vice Chair Barr is leading this,

21:40.266 --> 21:42.576 align:middle
and I think he's
testifying next week.

21:42.576 --> 21:44.436 align:middle
So-but that will be up to him.

21:45.086 --> 21:46.816 align:middle
So that's really where it is.

21:46.816 --> 21:49.836 align:middle
You know, the, the
review is going

21:49.836 --> 21:52.156 align:middle
to be thorough and transparent.

21:52.156 --> 21:54.986 align:middle
It is clear-really to your
last question-it's clear

21:54.986 --> 21:57.546 align:middle
that we do need to strengthen
supervision and regulation.

21:58.246 --> 22:02.436 align:middle
And I, I assume that there
will be recommendations coming

22:02.436 --> 22:04.496 align:middle
out of the report, and I,
I plan on supporting them

22:04.496 --> 22:05.746 align:middle
and supporting their
implementation.

22:06.956 --> 22:07.516 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

22:07.516 --> 22:09.956 align:middle
And the final point-you
know, can we feel confident

22:09.956 --> 22:11.836 align:middle
that these weaknesses
don't exist elsewhere,

22:11.836 --> 22:13.536 align:middle
given that they got
missed at this bank?

22:13.786 --> 22:14.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:14.246 --> 22:17.156 align:middle
These are not weaknesses that
are-that are at all broadly

22:17.156 --> 22:18.126 align:middle
through the banking system.

22:18.126 --> 22:20.416 align:middle
This was-this was a bank
that was an outlier in terms

22:20.416 --> 22:26.156 align:middle
of both its percentage of, of
uninsured deposits and in terms

22:26.156 --> 22:28.956 align:middle
of its holdings of
duration risk.

22:29.656 --> 22:32.556 align:middle
And again, supervisors did get
in there, and, and they were,

22:32.676 --> 22:36.886 align:middle
as you know, obviously, you
know they were-they were

22:36.886 --> 22:39.256 align:middle
on this issue, but nonetheless,
this, this still happened.

22:39.426 --> 22:42.036 align:middle
And so that's really the
nature of the interview-sorry,

22:42.036 --> 22:44.096 align:middle
of the review-is
to discover that.

22:44.096 --> 22:45.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:45.176 --> 22:46.436 align:middle
Let's go to Michael McKee.

22:46.436 --> 22:47.436 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

22:48.106 --> 22:50.226 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

22:50.526 --> 22:52.266 align:middle
You've been very
consistent in saying

22:52.266 --> 22:54.146 align:middle
that the Fed would be
raising interest rates

22:54.146 --> 22:56.226 align:middle
and then holding them
there for quite some time.

22:56.226 --> 22:59.646 align:middle
Following today's decision,
the markets have now priced

22:59.646 --> 23:03.526 align:middle
in one more increase in May,
and then every meeting the rest

23:03.526 --> 23:05.986 align:middle
of this year they're
pricing in rate cuts.

23:06.546 --> 23:10.446 align:middle
Are they getting this
totally wrong from the Fed,

23:10.446 --> 23:13.316 align:middle
or is there something different
about the way you're looking

23:13.316 --> 23:15.076 align:middle
at it, given that
you're now thinking

23:15.076 --> 23:18.856 align:middle
that moves might be appropriate
as opposed to ongoing?

23:18.856 --> 23:19.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:19.506 --> 23:22.816 align:middle
So we published an SEP today, as
you will have seen, and it shows

23:22.996 --> 23:26.686 align:middle
that basically participants
expect relatively slow growth,

23:27.246 --> 23:29.886 align:middle
a gradual rebalancing of supply
and demand in the labor market,

23:30.106 --> 23:31.956 align:middle
with inflation moving
down gradually.

23:32.916 --> 23:35.226 align:middle
In that most likely
case, if that happens,

23:35.476 --> 23:37.536 align:middle
participants don't see
rate cuts this year.

23:37.636 --> 23:38.146 align:middle
They just don't.

23:38.896 --> 23:40.136 align:middle
I would just say: As always,

23:40.136 --> 23:42.276 align:middle
the path of the economy is
uncertain, and policy is going

23:42.276 --> 23:43.816 align:middle
to reflect what actually
happens rather

23:43.816 --> 23:45.046 align:middle
than what we write
down in the SEP.

23:45.136 --> 23:47.116 align:middle
But that's not our
baseline expectation.

23:47.716 --> 23:48.286 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

23:48.366 --> 23:51.576 align:middle
Well, if I could follow up
and ask, as you look forward

23:51.576 --> 23:58.256 align:middle
into the rest of the year here,
are you saying that what you see

23:58.716 --> 24:03.276 align:middle
and the 5.1 percent
basically consensus is based

24:03.276 --> 24:06.566 align:middle
on being-it will be
sufficiently restrictive?

24:07.076 --> 24:09.016 align:middle
Or is it leavened by the idea

24:09.016 --> 24:10.626 align:middle
of you don't know
what's going to happen?

24:10.626 --> 24:13.126 align:middle
In other words, what should
people think about in terms

24:13.126 --> 24:16.576 align:middle
of how the Fed thinks about how
far it is from the terminal?

24:16.576 --> 24:16.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:16.996 --> 24:18.106 align:middle
It's going to depend.

24:18.236 --> 24:20.356 align:middle
Remember, we're looking-for
purposes

24:20.356 --> 24:22.446 align:middle
of our monetary policy
tool, we're looking

24:22.446 --> 24:26.656 align:middle
at what's happening among the
banks and asking, is there going

24:26.656 --> 24:28.426 align:middle
to be some tightening
in credit conditions?

24:28.426 --> 24:29.866 align:middle
And then we're thinking
about that

24:29.866 --> 24:32.626 align:middle
as effectively doing the same
thing that rate hikes do.

24:32.626 --> 24:34.906 align:middle
So, in a way, that
substitutes for rate hikes.

24:35.366 --> 24:38.966 align:middle
So, the key is, we have
to have-policy has got

24:38.966 --> 24:40.396 align:middle
to be tight enough
to bring inflation

24:40.396 --> 24:41.626 align:middle
down to 2 percent over time.

24:41.706 --> 24:45.266 align:middle
It doesn't all have to come from
rate hikes: It can come from,

24:45.266 --> 24:47.356 align:middle
you know, from tighter
credit conditions.

24:47.716 --> 24:49.466 align:middle
So we're looking at, and we,

24:49.526 --> 24:52.326 align:middle
we-it's highly uncertain how
long the situation will be

24:52.326 --> 24:54.716 align:middle
sustained or how significant
any of those effects would be,

24:54.716 --> 24:55.936 align:middle
so we're just going
to have to watch.

24:56.326 --> 24:59.916 align:middle
In the meantime,
you know-obviously,

24:59.916 --> 25:03.556 align:middle
at the end of the
day, we will do enough

25:03.986 --> 25:05.416 align:middle
to bring inflation
down to 2 percent.

25:05.416 --> 25:06.236 align:middle
No one should doubt that.

25:07.416 --> 25:07.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:07.886 --> 25:10.946 align:middle
Let's go to Rachel Siegel.

25:11.626 --> 25:12.826 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

25:13.616 --> 25:14.356 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

25:14.516 --> 25:15.916 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

25:15.916 --> 25:17.256 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

25:17.906 --> 25:18.796 align:middle
I know we've talked a bit

25:18.796 --> 25:21.736 align:middle
about how Silicon Valley Bank
was unique to a certain sector

25:21.736 --> 25:24.446 align:middle
of the economy, but
there's also growing concern

25:24.446 --> 25:25.836 align:middle
that there are financial
stability risks

25:25.836 --> 25:28.716 align:middle
from the commercial real estate
market and loans that will begin

25:28.716 --> 25:30.916 align:middle
to roll over later
this year and next,

25:31.316 --> 25:33.946 align:middle
and that smaller regional banks
also disproportionately hold

25:33.946 --> 25:34.596 align:middle
those loans.

25:35.206 --> 25:39.176 align:middle
Is there a risk that could mimic
the kind of-what we saw with SVB

25:39.176 --> 25:40.686 align:middle
to banks that disproportionately
are focused

25:40.686 --> 25:41.576 align:middle
in commercial real estate?

25:41.576 --> 25:42.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:42.286 --> 25:44.216 align:middle
So, you know, we're well aware

25:44.216 --> 25:46.986 align:middle
of the concentrations people
have in commercial real estate.

25:46.986 --> 25:49.036 align:middle
I really don't think
it's comparable to this.

25:49.036 --> 25:52.146 align:middle
The, the banking system
is, is strong, it is sound,

25:52.146 --> 25:55.236 align:middle
it is resilient, it's
well capitalized,

25:55.806 --> 26:00.596 align:middle
and I really don't see that
as at all analogous to this.

26:00.856 --> 26:01.216 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

26:01.216 --> 26:02.676 align:middle
And one other question:
Would you be open

26:02.676 --> 26:05.386 align:middle
to an independent investigation,
separate from the Fed's probe?

26:06.116 --> 26:06.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:06.536 --> 26:09.206 align:middle
I welcome-it's 100
percent certainty

26:09.206 --> 26:11.146 align:middle
that there will be
independent investigations

26:11.676 --> 26:13.356 align:middle
and outside investigations
and all that.

26:13.356 --> 26:17.946 align:middle
So we welcome-when a bank
fails, there are investigations.

26:17.946 --> 26:19.426 align:middle
And, of course, we welcome that.

26:19.566 --> 26:19.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:20.576 --> 26:21.466 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:23.046 --> 26:23.866 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

26:23.866 --> 26:25.516 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

26:25.956 --> 26:29.366 align:middle
Inflation has been rather
sticky, so do you need help

26:29.366 --> 26:32.056 align:middle
from the fiscal side to
get inflation down faster?

26:32.486 --> 26:33.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:33.636 --> 26:34.636 align:middle
We don't assume that.

26:35.066 --> 26:36.906 align:middle
We don't give advice to
the fiscal authorities,

26:36.906 --> 26:40.926 align:middle
and we assume that-we
take fiscal policy as,

26:40.926 --> 26:44.666 align:middle
as it comes to our front door,
stick it in our model along

26:44.736 --> 26:46.316 align:middle
with a million other things.

26:47.176 --> 26:48.916 align:middle
We have responsibility
for price stability.

26:49.066 --> 26:50.896 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has
responsibility for that.

26:51.016 --> 26:53.226 align:middle
And nothing's going
to change that.

26:53.326 --> 26:56.626 align:middle
So-and we will get inflation
down to 2 percent in time.

26:57.626 --> 26:58.006 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:58.006 --> 26:59.846 align:middle
And if I can follow on that,

26:59.936 --> 27:02.786 align:middle
but they're working-the spending
that's happened is working

27:02.786 --> 27:04.026 align:middle
against what you
are doing, right?

27:04.026 --> 27:05.806 align:middle
So it's prolonging inflation.

27:06.926 --> 27:07.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:07.206 --> 27:11.316 align:middle
You have to look at, at
the impulse from spending

27:11.316 --> 27:12.446 align:middle
because spending was, of course,

27:12.526 --> 27:14.566 align:middle
tremendously high during
the pandemic, and then,

27:14.566 --> 27:18.286 align:middle
as the pandemic programs
rolled off,

27:18.386 --> 27:20.846 align:middle
spending actually came
down, so the-this sort

27:20.846 --> 27:24.596 align:middle
of fiscal impulse is actually
not what's driving inflation

27:24.596 --> 27:25.006 align:middle
right now.

27:25.076 --> 27:26.996 align:middle
It was-it was at the
beginning perhaps part

27:26.996 --> 27:27.956 align:middle
of what was driving inflation,

27:27.956 --> 27:29.336 align:middle
but that's not really
the story now.

27:29.336 --> 27:29.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:30.846 --> 27:31.716 align:middle
Let's go to Neil Irwin.

27:32.136 --> 27:33.926 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

27:34.266 --> 27:35.046 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:35.046 --> 27:36.336 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

27:36.656 --> 27:39.016 align:middle
Two questions about aspects
of the government's response

27:39.016 --> 27:40.696 align:middle
on Silicon Valley
Bank two weekends ago.

27:41.326 --> 27:43.616 align:middle
First, why is this new
bank funding facility done

27:43.616 --> 27:47.286 align:middle
under emergency 13(3) authority,
as opposed to expansion

27:47.286 --> 27:48.606 align:middle
of the discount window,
changing the terms

27:48.606 --> 27:50.516 align:middle
of the discount window that's
been around a long time?

27:50.896 --> 27:52.686 align:middle
And, second, can you
discuss the Fed's role

27:52.686 --> 27:56.036 align:middle
in the-in the FDIC guarantee
of uninsured depositors

27:56.276 --> 27:59.646 align:middle
and why there's $143 billion on
your balance sheets last week,

27:59.886 --> 28:01.106 align:middle
supporting that deposit
guarantee?

28:01.416 --> 28:01.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:01.976 --> 28:07.426 align:middle
Sure. So 13(3) seemed like the
right-we have a little more

28:07.426 --> 28:09.606 align:middle
flexibility under section 13(3).

28:09.606 --> 28:12.536 align:middle
We've done quite a lot under
the discount window as well.

28:12.686 --> 28:14.246 align:middle
We needed to do a
special facility

28:14.246 --> 28:17.996 align:middle
that was designed a certain
way, so we did it under 13(3).

28:17.996 --> 28:18.976 align:middle
Really no magic to that.

28:18.976 --> 28:21.876 align:middle
It's only available in unusual
and exigent circumstances,

28:21.876 --> 28:24.866 align:middle
and it has to be-meet certain
requirements, but it seemed

28:24.866 --> 28:26.166 align:middle
to be the right place.

28:26.166 --> 28:30.876 align:middle
So with the FDIC, we're just,
we're lending to the, in effect,

28:30.876 --> 28:34.366 align:middle
we're-we're lending
to the bridge bank.

28:34.886 --> 28:36.556 align:middle
So that's where the
funds came from,

28:36.556 --> 28:39.466 align:middle
and it's-it's a loan that's 100
percent guaranteed by the FDIC,

28:39.466 --> 28:40.686 align:middle
so there's no risk in it for us.

28:42.386 --> 28:43.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:44.806 --> 28:45.586 align:middle
Okay, Chris Rugaber.

28:45.586 --> 28:46.276 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

28:46.276 --> 28:46.946 align:middle
Thank you.

28:46.946 --> 28:48.956 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

28:48.956 --> 28:52.986 align:middle
The SEB-SEP suggests one more
rate hike, as does the change

28:52.986 --> 28:56.856 align:middle
in the language in the
statement and which suggests

28:56.856 --> 29:00.546 align:middle
that you're perhaps nearing the
end of a cycle of rate hikes.

29:00.546 --> 29:02.686 align:middle
Do you feel, though, that
if inflation remains high,

29:02.686 --> 29:05.426 align:middle
you'll be able to resume
additional hikes as needed,

29:05.426 --> 29:08.786 align:middle
or have you somewhat tied your
hands here with these signals

29:08.786 --> 29:10.386 align:middle
about rate hikes
coming to an end?

29:10.386 --> 29:10.566 align:middle
Thank you.

29:10.566 --> 29:10.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:10.836 --> 29:11.526 align:middle
No, absolutely not.

29:11.526 --> 29:12.536 align:middle
No, we, if we need to raise
hike-raise rates higher,

29:12.616 --> 29:12.876 align:middle
we will.

29:12.876 --> 29:13.856 align:middle
I think for now though
we, we-as I've mentioned,

29:13.856 --> 29:15.146 align:middle
we see the likelihood
of, of credit tightening.

29:15.306 --> 29:17.466 align:middle
We know that can have, you know,
an effect on the macroeconomy,

29:17.466 --> 29:20.806 align:middle
on demand, on labor market, on
inflation, and we're-we're going

29:21.046 --> 29:22.526 align:middle
to be watching to
see what that is.

29:22.616 --> 29:25.176 align:middle
And we'll also be watching
what's happening with inflation

29:25.176 --> 29:26.056 align:middle
and in the labor market.

29:26.536 --> 29:28.246 align:middle
So we'll be watching
all those things,

29:28.896 --> 29:32.686 align:middle
and of course we will-we
will eventually get

29:32.686 --> 29:33.936 align:middle
to [a] tight enough policy

29:33.936 --> 29:35.316 align:middle
to bring inflation
down to 2 percent.

29:36.066 --> 29:37.466 align:middle
We'll find ourselves
at that place.

29:37.466 --> 29:37.966 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:37.966 --> 29:39.926 align:middle
Kyle. KYLE CAMPBELL.

29:41.876 --> 29:43.736 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell-thanks
for taking the question.

29:43.836 --> 29:45.326 align:middle
Kyle Campbell with
American Banker.

29:45.326 --> 29:47.776 align:middle
I have a couple questions
about the balance sheet.

29:48.166 --> 29:50.566 align:middle
First of all, I'm curious

29:50.566 --> 29:52.686 align:middle
at what point the
financial supports

29:52.686 --> 29:55.466 align:middle
that the Fed is extending
through the discount window

29:55.466 --> 29:59.526 align:middle
and through its enhanced lending
facility might be at odds

29:59.526 --> 30:02.306 align:middle
with the objective of
reducing the balance sheet.

30:02.726 --> 30:04.306 align:middle
And I'm also curious
what your thoughts are

30:04.376 --> 30:08.136 align:middle
on the-not just the availability
of reserves but the distribution

30:08.136 --> 30:10.156 align:middle
of them throughout
the banking system

30:10.156 --> 30:12.656 align:middle
and at what point you
might be concerned

30:12.656 --> 30:14.426 align:middle
about it being scarce
for certain banks.

30:15.356 --> 30:16.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:17.586 --> 30:20.536 align:middle
So-People think of QE
and QT in different ways,

30:20.536 --> 30:22.416 align:middle
so let me be clear
about how I'm thinking

30:22.416 --> 30:23.636 align:middle
about these recent developments.

30:24.516 --> 30:26.586 align:middle
So the recent liquidity
provision

30:26.586 --> 30:29.096 align:middle
that has increased the size of
our balance sheet but the intent

30:29.096 --> 30:32.306 align:middle
and the effects of it are very
different from what we-from

30:32.306 --> 30:34.756 align:middle
when we expand our balance
sheet through purchases

30:34.756 --> 30:36.156 align:middle
of longer-term securities.

30:36.736 --> 30:39.056 align:middle
Large-scale purchases of
long-term securities are,

30:39.056 --> 30:40.976 align:middle
are really meant to alter
the stance of policy

30:40.976 --> 30:44.686 align:middle
by pushing down-pushing up
the price and down the rates,

30:44.686 --> 30:46.576 align:middle
longer-term rates,
which supports demand

30:46.576 --> 30:48.336 align:middle
through channels we
understand fairly well.

30:49.186 --> 30:52.446 align:middle
The balance sheet expansion
is really temporary lending

30:52.446 --> 30:55.446 align:middle
to banks to meet those special
liquidity demands created

30:55.446 --> 30:57.806 align:middle
by the recent tensions;
it's not intended

30:57.806 --> 31:01.226 align:middle
to directly alter the
stance of monetary policy.

31:01.646 --> 31:02.926 align:middle
We do believe that it's working.

31:02.926 --> 31:05.256 align:middle
It's having its intended
effect of bolstering confidence

31:05.256 --> 31:06.086 align:middle
in the banking system

31:06.086 --> 31:08.656 align:middle
and thereby forestalling what
might otherwise have been an

31:08.656 --> 31:11.586 align:middle
abrupt and outsized tightening
in financial conditions.

31:11.586 --> 31:12.276 align:middle
So that's working.

31:12.686 --> 31:15.356 align:middle
In terms of the distribution
of reserves, we,

31:15.666 --> 31:21.836 align:middle
we don't see ourselves as, as
running into reserve shortages.

31:21.836 --> 31:25.676 align:middle
We, we think that our program of
allowing our balance sheet to,

31:25.746 --> 31:29.156 align:middle
to run off predictably,
predicatbly

31:29.156 --> 31:31.026 align:middle
and passively is working.

31:31.946 --> 31:35.116 align:middle
And of course we're,
we're always prepared to,

31:35.186 --> 31:36.476 align:middle
to change that if that changes.

31:36.476 --> 31:38.306 align:middle
But we don't see any
evidence that that's changed.

31:39.706 --> 31:42.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:43.026 --> 31:45.426 align:middle
Catarina. CATARINA SARAIVA.

31:45.426 --> 31:45.706 align:middle
Hi, Chair.

31:46.336 --> 31:47.766 align:middle
Catarina Saraiva
with Bloomberg News.

31:47.766 --> 31:50.316 align:middle
The minutes of the
January-February meeting,

31:50.316 --> 31:54.216 align:middle
the last meeting, indicate that
you discussed the possibility

31:54.216 --> 31:58.606 align:middle
of runs on nonbank financial
institutions and the impact

31:58.606 --> 32:01.646 align:middle
of large unrealized
losses on bank portfolios.

32:02.086 --> 32:04.896 align:middle
Can you talk a little bit more
about that discussion-kind

32:04.896 --> 32:08.396 align:middle
of what was talked
about in light of that,

32:08.956 --> 32:12.316 align:middle
and then why didn't the Fed,
you know, do anything about that

32:12.506 --> 32:15.226 align:middle
at that point to
ultimately prevent, you know,

32:15.226 --> 32:16.216 align:middle
what happened this month?

32:16.416 --> 32:16.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:16.866 --> 32:19.486 align:middle
I mean, to be honest, I don't-I
don't recall the specifics

32:19.606 --> 32:20.316 align:middle
of that.

32:20.316 --> 32:22.366 align:middle
It's been quite an
interesting seven weeks.

32:22.976 --> 32:25.256 align:middle
But, but I will tell
you, though,

32:25.256 --> 32:28.516 align:middle
that we have-there have
been presentations about,

32:28.646 --> 32:29.996 align:middle
about interest rate risk.

32:29.996 --> 32:31.486 align:middle
I mean, it's been in
all the newspapers.

32:31.486 --> 32:33.956 align:middle
It's not a surprise that
there are institutions

32:33.956 --> 32:37.286 align:middle
that have-that have had
unhedged long positions

32:37.286 --> 32:40.356 align:middle
in long-duration securities
that have lost value as,

32:40.356 --> 32:43.486 align:middle
as longer-term rates have gone
up due to our rate increases.

32:43.486 --> 32:44.766 align:middle
So that's, that's
not a surprise.

32:44.816 --> 32:50.486 align:middle
I, I think, as you know, as
is now in the public record,

32:51.176 --> 32:54.886 align:middle
the supervisory team
was apparently engaged,

32:55.006 --> 32:58.226 align:middle
very much engaged with
the bank repeatedly,

32:58.226 --> 33:00.806 align:middle
and was escalating but,
you know, nonetheless,

33:00.866 --> 33:01.676 align:middle
what happened happened.

33:01.676 --> 33:04.556 align:middle
And so that's really the
purpose of-one way to think

33:04.556 --> 33:08.206 align:middle
about the review that Vice Chair
Barr is conducting is to try

33:08.206 --> 33:10.226 align:middle
to understand how
that happened and try

33:10.226 --> 33:11.616 align:middle
to understand how
we can do better

33:11.616 --> 33:13.386 align:middle
and what policies
we need to change.

33:13.446 --> 33:16.916 align:middle
I mean, one thing is the speed
of the-I'll come back to that,

33:16.916 --> 33:22.076 align:middle
the speed of the run, it's very
different from what we've seen

33:22.076 --> 33:26.086 align:middle
in the past, and it does kind
of suggest that there's a need

33:26.086 --> 33:28.286 align:middle
for possible, you
know, regulatory

33:28.286 --> 33:30.686 align:middle
and supervisory changes
just because supervision

33:30.686 --> 33:32.056 align:middle
and regulation need to
keep up with what's,

33:32.056 --> 33:32.726 align:middle
what's happening in the world.

33:32.726 --> 33:33.376 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

33:33.976 --> 33:36.456 align:middle
Can you confirm whether
or not the Board knew

33:36.456 --> 33:39.586 align:middle
about these escalations by the
examiners in San Francisco?

33:39.646 --> 33:40.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:40.046 --> 33:43.586 align:middle
I will have to come
back to you on that.

33:43.586 --> 33:44.286 align:middle
Yeah, I don't know.

33:44.286 --> 33:45.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:45.196 --> 33:47.176 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

33:49.086 --> 33:50.886 align:middle
Hi. Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

33:50.886 --> 33:51.736 align:middle
Thank you very much.

33:52.436 --> 33:53.956 align:middle
Chair Powell, you
stated twice today

33:53.956 --> 33:57.446 align:middle
that all depositors' savings
in the banking system are safe.

33:57.636 --> 34:01.326 align:middle
Are you saying that de facto
deposit insurance covers

34:01.426 --> 34:02.356 align:middle
all savings?

34:03.146 --> 34:04.716 align:middle
Shouldn't Congress
have a say in that?

34:05.026 --> 34:07.866 align:middle
and, just by way of example, if
a bank with less than $1 billion

34:07.866 --> 34:10.536 align:middle
in assets failed, are
you promising to bail

34:10.536 --> 34:12.136 align:middle
out all of its depositors?

34:12.426 --> 34:12.996 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

34:13.036 --> 34:15.676 align:middle
Well, I'm not saying
anything more than I'm saying.

34:15.676 --> 34:20.526 align:middle
So-but what I'm saying is you've
seen that we have the tools

34:20.526 --> 34:25.086 align:middle
to protect depositors when
there's a threat of serious harm

34:25.086 --> 34:27.666 align:middle
to the economy or to-or
to the financial system,

34:28.296 --> 34:29.726 align:middle
and we're prepared
to use those tools.

34:30.376 --> 34:31.706 align:middle
And I think depositors
should assume

34:31.706 --> 34:34.136 align:middle
that their-that their
deposits are safe.

34:34.296 --> 34:34.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:34.956 --> 34:36.266 align:middle
Let's go to Greg Robb.

34:36.856 --> 34:38.816 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

34:39.086 --> 34:39.836 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

34:39.836 --> 34:40.876 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

34:41.246 --> 34:43.386 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
give us a little bit more color.

34:43.386 --> 34:44.786 align:middle
You gave just a little
bit of color.

34:44.786 --> 34:46.586 align:middle
You said during the first week

34:46.586 --> 34:49.556 align:middle
of the Silicon Valley
weekend-you said the question

34:49.556 --> 34:51.526 align:middle
you guys asked was
"How did this happen?"

34:51.526 --> 34:52.966 align:middle
when you saw Silicon
Valley Bank.

34:53.336 --> 34:56.636 align:middle
So I was wondering if you could
go to the Credit Suisse merger.

34:57.006 --> 34:59.156 align:middle
I mean, wasn't that the
big gorilla in the room?

34:59.196 --> 35:01.406 align:middle
Aren't-didn't you
breathe a sigh of relief

35:01.516 --> 35:04.276 align:middle
when that merger happened?

35:04.336 --> 35:05.666 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

35:05.666 --> 35:09.426 align:middle
Sure. So, you know, we-that
was really the Swiss government

35:09.606 --> 35:11.746 align:middle
that we of course were, were
following it over the course

35:11.746 --> 35:14.266 align:middle
of the weekend, and we were
engaged with their authorities

35:14.266 --> 35:15.756 align:middle
in the way that you would
expect, all the ways

35:15.756 --> 35:16.366 align:middle
that you would expect.

35:16.936 --> 35:19.416 align:middle
It seems to have been a
positive outcome in the sense

35:19.416 --> 35:22.136 align:middle
that the transaction
was agreed to,

35:22.136 --> 35:27.216 align:middle
and it has been-the markets
have accepted it, and it seems

35:27.216 --> 35:29.836 align:middle
to have gone well, and I
think there was a concern

35:29.836 --> 35:30.826 align:middle
that it might not go well.

35:30.826 --> 35:33.636 align:middle
So coming into the
middle of this week, yes,

35:33.856 --> 35:35.956 align:middle
I would say that that
is going well so far.

35:35.956 --> 35:36.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:38.646 --> 35:41.646 align:middle
Nicole. NICOLE GOODKIND.

35:43.916 --> 35:45.006 align:middle
Hi. Thank you, Chair Powell.

35:45.006 --> 35:46.676 align:middle
Nicole Goodkind with
CNN Business.

35:47.676 --> 35:49.566 align:middle
In the Summary of
Economic Projections,

35:49.566 --> 35:52.036 align:middle
the FOMC sees the
unemployment rate increasing

35:52.036 --> 35:53.736 align:middle
to 4.5 percent this year.

35:54.206 --> 35:56.346 align:middle
I'm wondering how you
anticipate preventing this

35:56.346 --> 35:59.446 align:middle
from snowballing while using
the admittedly blunt tools

35:59.446 --> 36:00.496 align:middle
at your disposal.

36:01.666 --> 36:01.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:01.936 --> 36:05.976 align:middle
So that's just-that's an
estimate of what will happen

36:05.976 --> 36:09.486 align:middle
as demand slows and
as conditions soften

36:09.486 --> 36:12.306 align:middle
in the labor market and
it's just-it's a highly

36:12.356 --> 36:13.216 align:middle
uncertain estimate.

36:13.886 --> 36:19.256 align:middle
And, I mean, I was
really-we have

36:19.256 --> 36:20.886 align:middle
to bring inflation
down to 2 percent.

36:21.726 --> 36:23.926 align:middle
The costs of bringing it
down-there are real costs

36:23.926 --> 36:25.406 align:middle
to bringing it down to
2 percent, but the costs

36:25.406 --> 36:26.596 align:middle
of failing are much higher.

36:26.876 --> 36:28.996 align:middle
And if you read your
history, as I'm sure you have,

36:29.546 --> 36:33.866 align:middle
you can see that if the central
bank doesn't get inflation back

36:33.866 --> 36:35.656 align:middle
in place, get inflation-make
sure

36:35.936 --> 36:37.836 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remain anchored,

36:38.286 --> 36:42.146 align:middle
you can have a long series of
years where inflation is high

36:42.146 --> 36:46.976 align:middle
and volatile, and it's
hard to invest capital,

36:46.976 --> 36:48.946 align:middle
it's hard for an
economy to perform well.

36:49.066 --> 36:53.106 align:middle
And we're looking to
avoid that and, you know,

36:53.106 --> 36:55.276 align:middle
to get back to where we need
to be-back to where we were

36:55.276 --> 36:58.626 align:middle
for a quarter century, and get
there as quickly as we can.

36:58.626 --> 36:59.696 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

36:59.696 --> 37:04.006 align:middle
But I guess the question
is, historically,

37:04.076 --> 37:09.436 align:middle
it's hard to-historically,
it's been hard

37:09.436 --> 37:13.666 align:middle
to contain unemployment and I,
I-the question is, do you worry

37:13.666 --> 37:16.076 align:middle
about some sort of snowball
effect, and how do you factor

37:16.076 --> 37:18.086 align:middle
that into your projections
and your thoughts?

37:18.136 --> 37:18.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:18.756 --> 37:22.736 align:middle
Well, it depends on whether
you-so recessions tend

37:22.736 --> 37:25.466 align:middle
to be nonlinear, and so
they're very hard to model.

37:25.466 --> 37:27.376 align:middle
You know, the models
all work in a kind

37:27.376 --> 37:29.896 align:middle
of linear way-if you have more
of this, you get more of that.

37:29.946 --> 37:33.126 align:middle
But when a recession
happens, the reactions tend

37:33.126 --> 37:36.506 align:middle
to be nonlinear and that's
what-so we don't know whether

37:36.506 --> 37:37.596 align:middle
that'll happen this time.

37:37.666 --> 37:41.006 align:middle
We don't know-if so, we don't
know how significant it will be,

37:41.426 --> 37:45.136 align:middle
and so, you know, we're very
focused on getting inflation

37:45.136 --> 37:48.686 align:middle
down because we know
in the longer run

37:48.686 --> 37:51.186 align:middle
that that is the thing that
will most benefit the people

37:51.186 --> 37:51.626 align:middle
we serve.

37:52.246 --> 37:54.246 align:middle
That's how we can
have a long-you know,

37:54.246 --> 37:56.016 align:middle
we've had very strong
labor markets

37:56.156 --> 37:58.436 align:middle
through these long
expansions that we've had.

37:58.786 --> 38:00.336 align:middle
Four of the five longest,

38:00.336 --> 38:02.036 align:middle
or three of the four
longest expansions

38:02.036 --> 38:06.046 align:middle
in U.S. history have been really
since the high-inflation period.

38:06.096 --> 38:09.346 align:middle
And the reason was inflation
wasn't forcing the central bank

38:09.346 --> 38:14.856 align:middle
to come in and stop an incipient
or, or, you know, an expansion.

38:15.286 --> 38:17.026 align:middle
You can have very,
very long expansions

38:17.026 --> 38:20.006 align:middle
without high inflation, and
we had several of those,

38:20.006 --> 38:21.416 align:middle
and they're very
good for people.

38:21.416 --> 38:24.896 align:middle
You see late in an expansion-you
see low unemployment,

38:24.896 --> 38:27.306 align:middle
you see the benefits of
wages going to people

38:27.746 --> 38:29.166 align:middle
at the lower end of
the wage spectrum.

38:29.166 --> 38:31.496 align:middle
It's just a place that we
should try to get back to.

38:33.166 --> 38:34.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:34.836 --> 38:36.066 align:middle
Jean. JEAN YUNG.

38:36.066 --> 38:36.516 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

38:36.516 --> 38:37.706 align:middle
Jean Young with Market News.

38:37.776 --> 38:40.956 align:middle
I just wanted to ask,
with all the events

38:40.956 --> 38:45.206 align:middle
of the past two weeks, do
you still see a possibility

38:45.206 --> 38:46.936 align:middle
of a soft landing
for the U.S. economy?

38:48.146 --> 38:48.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:49.676 --> 38:51.666 align:middle
You know it's, it's too
early to say, really,

38:51.666 --> 38:55.886 align:middle
whether these events have
had much of an effect.

38:55.886 --> 38:58.386 align:middle
It's hard for me to see how
they would have helped the

38:58.386 --> 39:01.066 align:middle
possibility-but I guess I
would just say, it's too early

39:01.066 --> 39:04.696 align:middle
to say whether there really
have been changes in that.

39:04.696 --> 39:07.456 align:middle
You know, the question will
be how long this period

39:07.456 --> 39:08.206 align:middle
is sustained.

39:08.446 --> 39:09.626 align:middle
The longer it's sustained,

39:10.226 --> 39:17.216 align:middle
then the greater will be the
likely declines in-or tightening

39:17.216 --> 39:21.196 align:middle
in credit standards,
credit availability,

39:21.346 --> 39:22.156 align:middle
so we'll just have to see.

39:22.156 --> 39:23.356 align:middle
I do still think, though,

39:23.356 --> 39:25.576 align:middle
that there's a-there's
a pathway to that.

39:25.576 --> 39:28.656 align:middle
I think that pathway still
exists and, you know,

39:28.656 --> 39:30.186 align:middle
we're certainly trying
to find it.

39:31.536 --> 39:31.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:32.106 --> 39:34.426 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:34.566 --> 39:35.216 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

39:35.216 --> 39:37.196 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

39:37.526 --> 39:40.446 align:middle
Just wondering: How many
financial institutions have been

39:40.446 --> 39:42.536 align:middle
issued matters requiring
attention

39:42.836 --> 39:46.206 align:middle
or matters requiring
immediate attention citations

39:46.206 --> 39:46.826 align:middle
at this point?

39:46.946 --> 39:47.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:47.226 --> 39:48.606 align:middle
How many? I don't know.

39:49.586 --> 39:51.796 align:middle
But those are serious-those
are serious regulatory,

39:51.796 --> 39:53.716 align:middle
in particular immediate
attention,

39:53.716 --> 39:56.566 align:middle
and that's-and I guess
there were six of them.

39:57.036 --> 39:58.106 align:middle
So. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:58.106 --> 40:00.006 align:middle
And, and getting to
the seriousness of it,

40:00.006 --> 40:04.046 align:middle
how are you going to
ensure that banks comply

40:04.046 --> 40:06.826 align:middle
with these citations, take
them seriously-how will you

40:06.826 --> 40:07.336 align:middle
enforce them?

40:07.656 --> 40:07.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:07.936 --> 40:10.136 align:middle
That is a great question
and is right in the heart

40:10.356 --> 40:13.796 align:middle
of what the review will be doing

40:13.796 --> 40:15.186 align:middle
under Vice Chair
Barr's leadership.

40:15.356 --> 40:18.906 align:middle
So that's, I think that's
where-that's what you

40:18.906 --> 40:19.516 align:middle
think about.

40:19.516 --> 40:22.626 align:middle
What can we do to make
sure that-but, again,

40:22.626 --> 40:24.316 align:middle
that's not for me
to answer today.

40:24.316 --> 40:24.966 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

40:24.996 --> 40:25.856 align:middle
Do you have specific
thoughts on that?

40:25.856 --> 40:26.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:26.066 --> 40:29.036 align:middle
Well I, I-see, if I did, I
wouldn't share them because I,

40:29.506 --> 40:34.146 align:middle
I really, you know, this review
is going on, and, you know,

40:34.146 --> 40:38.826 align:middle
I want nothing other than us to
find out what happened and why,

40:39.856 --> 40:41.866 align:middle
figure out what we
can do to do better,

40:42.076 --> 40:43.236 align:middle
and then implement
those changes.

40:43.236 --> 40:44.016 align:middle
That's all I want.

40:44.016 --> 40:46.826 align:middle
It's-For me to be giving
you my half-formed,

40:46.826 --> 40:49.406 align:middle
or partially informed,
thoughts, it, you know,

40:49.446 --> 40:50.726 align:middle
just isn't appropriate.

40:50.726 --> 40:53.236 align:middle
There's a real serious
review going on with,

40:53.236 --> 40:55.286 align:middle
with people from all over
the Federal Reserve System

40:55.286 --> 40:58.736 align:middle
who are not connected to
this, you know, to this work,

40:59.146 --> 41:02.266 align:middle
not connected to this
bank, and under, again,

41:02.366 --> 41:04.826 align:middle
Vice Chair Barr's
leadership, and I'm confident

41:04.826 --> 41:06.786 align:middle
that it will produce
a satisfactory result.

41:07.726 --> 41:08.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:08.106 --> 41:09.576 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to Jennifer
for the last question.

41:09.866 --> 41:11.196 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

41:11.466 --> 41:12.376 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

41:12.376 --> 41:14.246 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

41:14.846 --> 41:17.706 align:middle
Curious-how do you view
financial conditions right now?

41:17.836 --> 41:21.466 align:middle
If credit becomes expensive
enough, choking off growth,

41:21.466 --> 41:22.886 align:middle
as you said you're watching for,

41:23.296 --> 41:25.896 align:middle
would that situation
warrant a rate cut?

41:25.896 --> 41:28.306 align:middle
What situation would
warrant a rate cut?

41:28.306 --> 41:31.176 align:middle
And have the bank failures
prompted any discussion

41:31.176 --> 41:32.916 align:middle
around changing the
implementation

41:32.916 --> 41:34.276 align:middle
of the balance sheet runoff?

41:34.426 --> 41:34.666 align:middle
Thank you.

41:34.666 --> 41:35.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:35.566 --> 41:36.986 align:middle
So we haven't really talked

41:36.986 --> 41:41.036 align:middle
about changing the balance
sheet implementation-that's not

41:41.036 --> 41:42.336 align:middle
something we've discussed yet.

41:42.686 --> 41:44.486 align:middle
As I mentioned, we're
always willing to change

41:44.516 --> 41:46.006 align:middle
that if we conclude
that it's appropriate,

41:46.006 --> 41:47.736 align:middle
but we're really not
seeing any signs there.

41:48.376 --> 41:50.516 align:middle
Sorry, then the question
before that was,

41:50.696 --> 41:53.026 align:middle
just give me a- JENNIFER
SCHONBERGER.

41:53.196 --> 41:56.266 align:middle
Curious how you view
financial conditions now and,

41:56.266 --> 41:57.676 align:middle
if credit were to
tighten enough,

41:57.676 --> 41:59.456 align:middle
if that would prompt a rate cut.

41:59.636 --> 42:00.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:02.056 --> 42:05.996 align:middle
So financial conditions seem
to have tightened-and probably

42:05.996 --> 42:10.246 align:middle
by more than the
traditional indexes say,

42:10.396 --> 42:13.526 align:middle
because traditional
indexes are focused a lot

42:13.526 --> 42:14.936 align:middle
on [interest] rates and
[prices of] equities,

42:15.426 --> 42:19.676 align:middle
and they don't necessarily
capture lending conditions.

42:19.846 --> 42:21.476 align:middle
So we think that, though.

42:21.476 --> 42:24.296 align:middle
So there are other measures
which, if they're focused on,

42:24.846 --> 42:26.506 align:middle
you know-bank lending
conditions and things

42:26.506 --> 42:28.156 align:middle
like that-they show
some more tightening.

42:28.436 --> 42:31.876 align:middle
The question for us, though,
is, how significant will that be

42:31.876 --> 42:33.936 align:middle
and how, you know-what
will be the extent of it

42:33.936 --> 42:35.676 align:middle
and what will be
the duration of it?

42:36.236 --> 42:37.406 align:middle
And then-and then, you know,

42:37.406 --> 42:40.476 align:middle
once you have-once you know
that, there's a fair amount

42:40.476 --> 42:44.956 align:middle
of research about how that,
with broad uncertainty bands-how

42:44.956 --> 42:46.736 align:middle
that works its way
into the economy [and]

42:46.736 --> 42:47.756 align:middle
over what period of time.

42:47.756 --> 42:51.126 align:middle
And so, you know, we'll be
looking to see the first part

42:51.126 --> 42:53.806 align:middle
of that-like how serious
is this, and does it look

42:53.806 --> 42:56.526 align:middle
like it's going to be sustained,
and if it is, you know,

42:56.606 --> 42:59.616 align:middle
it could easily have a
significant macroeconomic

42:59.616 --> 43:02.846 align:middle
effect, and we would factor
that into our policy decisions.

43:06.056 --> 43:09.216 align:middle
I mentioned with rate cuts, rate
cuts are not in our base case,

43:09.716 --> 43:15.666 align:middle
and, you know, so
that's all I have to say.

43:15.756 --> 43:18.596 align:middle
Thank you very much.

